{
  "dom_id": "step-q148371",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=-10$ | $Y=0$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-20$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=-14\\,\\text{E}[Y]=-7\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈9.1652,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈4.5826,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(-20)\\cdot (-10)\\cdot 0.6+(-20)\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & +0\\cdot (-10)\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =120-(-14)(-7) \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{22}{9.1652\\cdot 4.5826} \\\\  & ≈0.52,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
